  <h1>Exploring the Fibonnaci Sequence and Golden Ratio</h1>
  <p class="p-date">Nov 15, 2025</p>
  <img src="OIG3.jpg" style="max-width: 100%">
  <p>The Fibonacci sequence is one of the most well-known integer sequences in mathematics, appearing in fields ranging from number theory to biology and art. Defined by a simple recurrence, it exhibits remarkably deep structure. In this article, we examine its behavior and establish its connection to the golden ratio, a number long associated with aesthetically pleasing proportions.</p>

  <p>
    The Fibonacci sequence is defined by the initial values  
    \( F_0 = 0 \) and \( F_1 = 1 \), and the recurrence relation  
    \( F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \).
  </p>

  <p>Binet's formula expresses \( F_n \) in closed form:</p>

  $$F_n = \frac{\phi^n - (1-\phi)^n}{\sqrt{5}}$$

  <p>
    where the constant \( \phi = \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2} \)  
    is known as the golden ratio.
  </p>
<pre><code>
<span class="keyword">def</span> <span class="function">greet</span>(<span class="punct">name</span>):
    <span class="keyword">print</span>(<span class="string">f"Hello, {name}!"</span>)

<span class="function">greet</span>(<span class="string">"World"</span>)
</code></pre>
  <p>
    The ratio of consecutive Fibonacci numbers approaches the golden ratio:
  </p>

  $$\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n} = \phi.$$



